event('236-1.5-characteristic-counterexample','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement 236-1.5-invertible adds n invertible on S. Counterexample to the printed unrestricted statement: in characteristic p let A=k[t,x,x^{-1}]/(t(x-1)), with the multiplicative Hopf structure on x, over S=Spec k[t]. The relation is a Hopf ideal. Every nonzero-t fibre is the trivial group and the zero fibre is G_m, so there is no geometric additive subgroup. The p-power endomorphism fixes t and sends x to x^p; it is finite, radicial and surjective, hence a universal homeomorphism and universally open. Yet the open D(x-1) is nonempty and has image only {t=0}, so the structure map is not open. Fibre dimension also jumps from zero to one.','236, pp. 227-228, corollary 1.5 first assertion and dimension consequence')
event('236-1.5-invertible','targeted proof check','With n invertible on the base, the stated density input is the density of n-power torsion in geometric connected commutative groups without an additive part and in the corresponding n-primary component locus. The kernel is a base change of multiplication by n, so is universally open over S; the same holds for n^h. A neighbourhood meeting the dense fibrewise union of these kernels therefore has image containing a neighbourhood of its base point. This verifies the openness mechanism, conditional on the classical structure/density theorem. The local constancy of fibre dimension also uses the dimension theorem for a universally open finite-type morphism at the unit. Those two inputs are recorded as background rather than independently proved.','236, p. 228, corollary 1.5 and proof')
event('236-1.6-dominance','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined statement 236-1.6-component-dominance says that each irreducible component dominates a component of the base, universally after base change. The printed assertion that each component dominates the whole base fails for the identity S to S when S is the disjoint union of two points (or a reducible connected scheme). The universal-openness definition itself is correct; the component criterion and discrete-valuation test require their usual finite-type topological results, which are background here.','236, p. 228, remark 1.6 opening')
event('236-1.6-coarse-j-line','refuted, with the refined statement\'s id','Refined example 236-1.6-fine-modular-base uses a fine modular curve over the coarse j-line. A smooth elliptic family over a neighbourhood of j=0 in the coarse line with the tautological j-invariant would have a local Weierstrass model with invertible discriminant and j=c4^3/Delta. Its positive vanishing order would be divisible by three, contradicting the simple zero of the coordinate j. Thus the literal printed universal-family base is not available. The fine-moduli replacement supplies the intended family; Picard-component jumps from extra complex-multiplication endomorphisms are classical background, not established by this local obstruction.','236, p. 228, remark 1.6 Picard example')
